 ## Overview
-TT4D is a memory- and time-efficient lossy compression technique for four-dimensional (4D) time-varying volume data based on the Tensor-Train (TT) decomposition [S1][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of interactive visualization of large-scale, time-varying volumetric data by enabling random-access decoding of individual time steps without reconstructing the entire 4D tensor into memory. It sets itself apart from its siblings, such as [Value compression](6-value-compression.md) and [Blockwise Wavelet Compression](6100-blockwise-wavelet-compression.md), by guaranteeing fixed-rate compression and random-access patterns through linear tensor cores, avoiding variable-bit length encoding or vector quantization [S1][S2, unofficial]. Unlike [Wavelet and Pyramid Transforms](3856-wavelet-and-pyramid-transforms.md) which rely on hierarchical subbands, TT4D factorizes the data into a chain of low-order cores, allowing for efficient subsampling during decomposition and on-the-fly GPU-based reconstruction [S1][S2, unofficial].
+TT4D is a memory- and time-efficient lossy compression technique for four-dimensional (4D) time-varying volume data based on the Tensor-Train (TT) decomposition [S1][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of interactive visualization of large-scale, time-varying volumetric data by enabling random-access decoding of individual time steps without reconstructing the entire 4D tensor into memory [S1][S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its siblings, such as [Value compression](6-value-compression.md) and [Blockwise Wavelet Compression](6100-blockwise-wavelet-compression.md), by guaranteeing fixed-rate compression and random-access patterns through linear tensor cores, avoiding variable-bit length encoding or vector quantization [S1][S2, unofficial]. Unlike [Wavelet and Pyramid Transforms](3856-wavelet-and-pyramid-transforms.md) which rely on hierarchical subbands, TT4D factorizes the data into a chain of low-order cores, allowing for efficient subsampling during decomposition and on-the-fly GPU-based reconstruction [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
